.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
.TH DIASPORA 1 2026-11-21 "diaspora 0.1" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
diaspora \- bring fixes over from peer repositories, picked, ported and tested
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diaspora
.RB [ \-C
.IR dir ]
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ peer ...]
.PP
.B diaspora auto
.RB [ \-\-yes | \-\-dry\-run | \-\-cron ]
.RB [ \-\-stage\-size
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-review ]
.RI [ peer ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diaspora
treats every repository that shares history with yours as a
.IR peer .
It lists the commits a peer has that you do not, sorts them by kind
(bugfix, feature, docs, ...), with an AI agent's opinion where the commit
message is not enough, and imports the ones you pick, one by one: each is
cherry\-picked on an import branch in a separate worktree, ported by the
agent where it does not apply, tested at the levels
.I diaspora.conf
requires for its kind, and, if it passes, copied to your target branch
with a
.B Diaspora\-Origin:
trailer.
Verdicts go into
.IR diaspora.ledger ,
and what was tried and did not work into
.IR diaspora.log ,
both committed next to
.IR diaspora.conf .
.PP
Nothing reaches the target branch untested, and diaspora never pushes.
.PP
With no command,
.B import
is run.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR import " [" \fIpeer\fR ...]
Fetch, scan and list what the peers have for us; pick on the terminal;
import, test and promote what passes.
.TP
.BR auto " [" \fIpeer\fR ...]
Propose a staged import plan and carry it out once agreed; see
.B AUTO\-IMPORT
below.
.TP
.BR list " [" \fIpeer\fR ...]
List the candidates and stop
.RB ( \-\-json
for a program).
.TP
.BI export " peer"
Put our commits that the peer lacks, and that we are willing to offer, on
a branch based on the peer's, ready to push for a pull request.
.TP
.B status
Peers, ledger counts and the run in progress.
.TP
.BI show " commit"
A candidate: message, files, triage, verdict and earlier attempts
.RB ( \-\-diff
for the diff).
.TP
.BR continue ", " skip ", " abort
For a run that stopped (a conflict, a failing baseline): go on after
resolving it, leave the waiting commit out, or drop the run.
.B abort \-\-no\-log
also drops what would go into
.IR diaspora.log .
.TP
.B promote
Copy a finished run to the target branch.
.BI \-\-include " commit"
takes a commit that failed its tests anyway;
.BI \-\-reject " commit"
and
.BI \-\-defer " commit"
leave a failed commit out with that verdict instead of
.IR failed .
.TP
.BR reject ", " defer ", " reopen ", " withhold " " \fIcommit\fR ...
Record a verdict without importing:
never suggest it again, hide it until
.BR \-\-all ,
forget the verdict, never offer this commit of ours to a peer.
.B \-m
gives the reason.
.TP
.BR test " [" \fIlevel\fR ...]
Run test levels on the target branch in the workspace.
.TP
.BR gui " [" \fIpeer\fR ...]
The pick list and the run in a browser page on 127.0.0.1: accept, reject,
defer, retry, auto\-import.
.TP
.B fetch
Fetch the peer branches into
.IR refs/diaspora/ .
.TP
.B log
Print the ledger.
.TP
.B repos
The repositories listed in
.IR ~/.config/diaspora ,
with any run in progress.
.TP
.B init
Write a starting
.I diaspora.conf
from the repository's remotes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-C " dir"
Run as if started in
.IR dir ,
or in the repository of that name in
.IR ~/.config/diaspora .
.PP
For
.BR import ,
.BR auto ,
.B list
and
.BR export :
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fetch
Use the peer branches as last fetched.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-agent
No AI agent: classify from commit messages only; a conflict stops the run
.RB ( import )
or fails the commit
.RB ( auto ).
.TP
.BI \-\-target " branch"
The branch to import into (default: the
.B target
in
.IR diaspora.conf ,
else the current branch).
.B \-\-here
imports into the current branch whatever
.I diaspora.conf
says.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also list ignored and deferred commits.
.TP
.BI \-\-max " n"
List at most
.I n
commits (0: no limit; default 50).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show test and agent output as it runs.
.PP
For
.B import
and
.BR export :
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask: take the suggested commits and promote what passes.
.TP
.BI \-\-pick " commit"
Take this commit (repeatable); no list.
.TP
.BR \-\-strategy " " each | batch
Test each import on its own, or all together.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-promote
Leave the result on its import branch.
.SH AUTO-IMPORT
.B diaspora auto
scans as
.B import
does, then proposes a plan instead of a pick list: the commits diaspora
would suggest anyway, in stages, the safest first:
.IP 1. 4
suggested commits that touch none of the files our own commits changed;
.IP 2. 4
suggested commits that touch files we changed too, whose overlap is not
known, or that the agent calls high\-risk;
.IP 3. 4
with
.BR \-\-review ,
commits listed for review that the agent says to import.
.PP
A commit goes no earlier than a commit it needs, and is left out when what
it needs is.
Commits that failed before or were tried and did not work are never in a
plan (they need a Retry, with someone looking), nor are deferred or
ignored commits, nor commits of a run in progress.
.PP
Each stage is an ordinary import run: what passes is promoted to the
target before the next stage starts; a commit that fails its tests, or
whose conflict the agent cannot resolve, is left out and offered again.
The plan stops when a run cannot go on by itself (the tests cannot run, or
fail on the target without the import); that run then waits for
.BR "diaspora continue" ,
.B promote
or
.BR abort .
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Carry the plan out without asking.
Without it,
.B auto
asks on a terminal and only shows the plan otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show the plan and stop (also while a run is in progress).
.TP
.BI \-\-stage\-size " n"
At most
.I n
commits per stage, split evenly (0: no limit; default 8).
.TP
.B \-\-review
Add the third stage.
.TP
.B \-\-cron
Unattended, for
.BR cron (8);
implies
.BR \-\-yes .
See
.B RUNNING FROM CRON
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " file"
With
.BR \-\-cron :
where the full output goes.
.PP
Only one
.B diaspora auto
(or the page's Carry out) runs at a time in a repository; a second one
says so and stops.
.SH RUNNING FROM CRON
.B diaspora auto \-\-cron
is made to be run by
.BR cron (8),
which mails whatever a job prints:
.IP \(bu 2
When there is nothing to import, it prints nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
When it carried out a plan, it prints one line per planned commit with
what became of it
.RI ( applied ,
.IR adapted ,
.IR failed ,
.IR superseded ,
.IR "not run" ),
the range now on the target, whether the plan stopped and why, and the
log file.
.IP \(bu 2
When a run is waiting for a person (a conflict, a failing baseline, a
run that was interrupted), it imports nothing and says so, every time,
until the run is finished.
.IP \(bu 2
When the previous
.B diaspora auto
in that repository is still going (a full regression can take hours), or
another run is at work there (started from the page, say), it exits
quietly.
.IP \(bu 2
Errors go to standard error, with the log file.
.PP
Everything else (the scan, the plan, each stage, the test and agent
output) goes to the log: by default a new file in
.IR ~/.cache/diaspora/<repo>\-<hash>/auto/ ,
of which the last 30 are kept.
.PP
cron starts jobs with almost no environment.
The test commands in
.I diaspora.conf
need what they get on your terminal (their tools on
.BR PATH ,
any variables a level relies on), and the agent
.RB ( claude )
must be installed and logged in for the user the job runs as.
A small script that sets these up and then runs diaspora is the easy way;
.I examples/cron/
in the diaspora sources has one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done (or nothing to do).
.TP
.B 1
Error; for
.B auto
without
.BR \-\-cron ,
also a run in progress or another
.B auto
running.
.TP
.B 3
Waiting for a person: a conflict, a baseline that fails, a plan that
stopped at a run.
.TP
.B 4
.RB ( import )
Some imports failed.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR DIASPORA_SRC ", " DIASPORA_BUILD
Set for test commands: the source tree under test and its build
directory.
.TP
.B DIASPORA_SCHEMA
Set for the agent: the JSON schema of the answer it must print.
.TP
.BR DIASPORA_CONFIG ", " XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the user's list of repositories is.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where workspaces and cron logs go (default
.IR ~/.cache ).
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
No colour on a terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I diaspora.conf
Peers, policy, test levels and what testing each kind of change requires;
committed.
.TP
.I diaspora.local.conf
Settings for this machine only; not committed.
.TP
.IR diaspora.ledger ", " diaspora.log
Verdicts, and what was tried and did not work; committed by diaspora.
.TP
.I ~/.config/diaspora
The repositories diaspora looks after
.RB ( "diaspora \-C" " " \fIname\fR ),
and the agent to use.
.TP
.I .git/diaspora/
The run in progress
.RI ( session.json ),
the triage cache, agent prompts and answers, the
.B auto
lock.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/diaspora/<repo>\-<hash>/
The workspace
.RI ( src ", " build )
and cron logs
.RI ( auto/ ).
.TP
.IR refs/diaspora/ <peer>/<branch>
The fetched peer branches.
.TP
.IR diaspora/import/ "<time>, " diaspora/export/ <peer>/<time>
Import and export branches.
.SH EXAMPLES
Look at what the peers have, pick and import:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cd ~/src/nvc
diaspora
.fi
.RE
.PP
See the plan for an unattended import, without carrying it out:
.PP
.RS
.nf
diaspora \-C VACASK auto \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Carry it out, smaller stages, without asking:
.PP
.RS
.nf
diaspora \-C VACASK auto \-\-yes \-\-stage\-size 4
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every night at 01:30, through a script that sets up the environment
first (cron mails only when something was imported, or went wrong):
.PP
.RS
.nf
MAILTO=me@example.org
30 1 * * *  $HOME/bin/diaspora\-cron VACASK xyce
.fi
.RE
.PP
where
.I diaspora\-cron
is, at its simplest:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#!/bin/sh
\&. "$HOME/.profile"
for repo in "$@"; do diaspora \-C "$repo" auto \-\-cron; done
.fi
.RE
.PP
Have an agent session drive an import:
.PP
.RS
.nf
diaspora list \-\-json
diaspora import \-\-pick 3cb89e1f0f \-\-no\-agent   # exit 3: conflict
(resolve it in the workspace, git add)
diaspora continue
diaspora promote
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git\-cherry\-pick (1),
.BR cron (8),
.BR crontab (5).
.PP
The README in the diaspora sources, https://github.com/kev\-cam/diaspora,
covers
.I diaspora.conf
in full.
